WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.529
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:09.800
In a previous episode, we discussed a technique
where we could specify a low-quality image

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.670
of a test subject, and a photo of a different
person.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:19.200
What happens then is that the algorithm transformed
our test subject into that pose.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.300
With an other algorithm, we can transfer our
facial gestures onto a different target subject.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:31.130
And this new method does something completely
different — here, we can copy a full-body

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.810
movement from a video and transfer it onto
a target person.

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:42.140
This way, we can appear to be playing tennis,
baseball or finally be able to perform a hundred

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.140
chin-ups.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.969
Well, at least, on Instagram.

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:48.100
Now, look here!

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.960
Up here you see the target poses, and on the
left, the target subjects.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.809
And between them, we see the output of this
algorithm with the target subjects taking

00:00:57.809 --> 00:00:59.309
these poses.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:05.059
As you see, the algorithm is quite consistent
in a sense that during walking, we often encounter

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:08.720
the same pose, which results in a very similar
image.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.960
That’s exactly the kind of consistency that
we’re looking for!

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.280
Remarkably, this algorithm is also able to
synthesize angles of these target subjects

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.450
that it hadn’t seen before.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.490
For instance, the backside of this person
was never shown to the algorithm and it correctly

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:31.820
guesses interesting details, like the belt
of this character to continue around the waist.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.740
Really cool, I love it!

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:38.250
We can also put these characters in a virtual
environment and animate them there.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:45.200
Now, this work, like most papers that explore
something completely new, is raw and experimental.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.090
Clearly, there are issues with occlusions,
flickering, and the silhouettes of the characters

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.579
give the trick away.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:56.960
Anyone looking at this footage can tell in
a second that it is not real.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.820
The reason I am so excited about this is that
now, we finally see that this is a viable

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.970
concept, and it will provide fertile grounds
for new followup research works to be improved

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:08.970
upon.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:14.549
Two more papers down the line, it will probably
work in HD and look significantly better.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:21.209
Just imagine how amazing that would be for
movies, computer games and telepresence applications.

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:22.459
Sign me up!

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:27.469
And, computer graphics research has a vast
body of papers on how to illuminate these

00:02:27.469 --> 00:02:32.309
characters to appear as if they were really
there in this environment.

00:02:32.309 --> 00:02:35.629
Will this be done with computer graphics,
or through AI?

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:40.669
I am really keen to see how these fields will
come together to solve such a challenging

00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:41.669
problem.

00:02:41.669 --> 00:02:43.060
What a time to be alive!

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.579
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

